/-! # Structure-sheaf cohomology of a plane hypersurface

Source: Stacks Project, Tag 0BYD, proof of lemma-genus-plane-curve;
Tag 0BYA, plane curves. (blueprint: lem-sfb-plane-hypersurface-cohomology)

The hypersurface is the zero scheme of the actual homogeneous section F of O(d).
On D_+(X_i) its ideal is (F/X_i^d), so this is Proj(k[A,B,C]/(F)),
with its closed subscheme structure even when F has repeated factors.
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits

namespace AlgebraicGeometry.PlaneHypersurface

/-- The ideal sheaf generated by the equation F, without taking a radical.
Source: Stacks 0BYA, the closed plane hypersurface defined by its homogeneous equation;
0BYD, the quotient sheaf in the hypersurface exact sequence.
(blueprint: lem-sfb-plane-hypersurface-cohomology) -/
noncomputable def ideal {k : Type u} [Field k] (d : ℕ)
    (F : MvPolynomial (ULift.{u} (Fin 3)) k) (hF : F.IsHomogeneous d) :
    (Proj.projectiveSpaceOverAffine (ULift.{u} (Fin 3)) k).IdealSheafData :=
  letI := MvPolynomial.gradedAlgebra (σ := ULift.{u} (Fin 3)) (R := k)
  Scheme.Modules.Hom.zeroLocus
    (Proj.unitToTwist ((MvPolynomial.mem_homogeneousSubmodule _ _).mpr hF))

/-- Proj(k[A,B,C]/(F)), realized as the zero scheme of F on Proj(k[A,B,C]).
In the coordinate chart D_+(X_i), the section F has coefficient F/X_i^d;
thus its zero scheme is Spec((k[A,B,C]_{X_i})_0/(F/X_i^d)). These are precisely
all three standard charts of Proj(k[A,B,C]/(F)), with the same localization restrictions.
Source: Stacks 0BYA and 0BYD, the given plane hypersurface.
(blueprint: lem-sfb-plane-hypersurface-cohomology) -/
noncomputable def scheme {k : Type u} [Field k] (d : ℕ)
    (F : MvPolynomial (ULift.{u} (Fin 3)) k) (hF : F.IsHomogeneous d) : Scheme.{u} :=
  (ideal d F hF).subscheme

/-- The specified closed immersion of the hypersurface into the projective plane.
Source: Stacks 0BYD, the closed immersion i:Z -> P^2_k.
(blueprint: lem-sfb-plane-hypersurface-cohomology) -/
noncomputable def ι {k : Type u} [Field k] (d : ℕ)
    (F : MvPolynomial (ULift.{u} (Fin 3)) k) (hF : F.IsHomogeneous d) :
    scheme d F hF ⟶ Proj.projectiveSpaceOverAffine (ULift.{u} (Fin 3)) k :=
  (ideal d F hF).subschemeι

/-- The structure morphism to Spec(k), induced by the coefficient homomorphism.
Source: Stacks 0BYA, plane curves over k.
(blueprint: lem-sfb-plane-hypersurface-cohomology) -/
noncomputable def π {k : Type u} [Field k] (d : ℕ)
    (F : MvPolynomial (ULift.{u} (Fin 3)) k) (hF : F.IsHomogeneous d) :
    scheme d F hF ⟶ Spec (.of k) :=
  ι d F hF ≫ Proj.projectiveSpaceOverAffineToSpec (ULift.{u} (Fin 3)) k

/-- The canonical scalar map k -> Gamma(Z,O_Z), hence to H^0(Z,O_Z).
Source: Stacks 0BYD, the first cohomology isomorphism, given by restriction of constants.
(blueprint: lem-sfb-plane-hypersurface-cohomology) -/
noncomputable def scalarMap {k : Type u} [Field k] (d : ℕ)
    (F : MvPolynomial (ULift.{u} (Fin 3)) k) (hF : F.IsHomogeneous d) :
    k →+* Γ(scheme d F hF, ⊤) :=
  ((Scheme.ΓSpecIso (.of k)).inv ≫ (π d F hF).appTop).hom

/-- H^q(Z,O_Z), with its k-vector-space structure induced by the scalar map.
Source: Stacks 0BYD; module cohomology as right derived global sections, Stacks 01DZ.
(blueprint: lem-sfb-plane-hypersurface-cohomology) -/
noncomputable def cohomology {k : Type u} [Field k] (d : ℕ)
    (F : MvPolynomial (ULift.{u} (Fin 3)) k) (hF : F.IsHomogeneous d) (q : ℕ) :
    ModuleCat.{u} k :=
  (ModuleCat.restrictScalars (scalarMap d F hF)).obj
    ((RingedSpace.moduleCohomology (scheme d F hF).toRingedSpace q).obj
      (Scheme.Modules.unit (scheme d F hF)))

/-- A nonzero degree-d plane equation has only constant global functions, and
its first structure-sheaf cohomology has dimension (d-1)(d-2)/2 over k.
Bijectivity is asserted for the canonical scalar ring map, so it is an isomorphism
of k-algebras for the algebra structure that this map induces. Global sections
are H^0(Z,O_Z), with its usual multiplication. Cardinal-valued `Module.rank`
expresses the dimension, including finiteness, without the infinite-dimension
junk value of `Module.finrank`.
Source: Stacks 0BYD, proof of lemma-genus-plane-curve, using only a nonzero
positive-degree homogeneous equation; Section 0BYA.
(blueprint: lem-sfb-plane-hypersurface-cohomology)

Proof, following the seven DAG steps:
1. In S=k[A,B,C], multiplication by F is injective because S is a domain
   (lem-weil-pbc-polynomial-ring-domain). The shifted grading of
   def-weil-pbc-proj-twisting-sheaf makes S(-d) -> S degree preserving, with
   image (F). Its cokernel is S/(F), giving the graded short exact sequence.
2. Apply lem-sfb-proj-graded-sheafification-exact. On the coordinate charts,
   S and S(-d) sheafify to O and O(-d); the generators X_i^(-d) have transition
   X_i^(-d)=(X_i/X_j)^(-d) X_j^(-d), as in def-pc-projective-space-twist.
   The fraction formula of lem-sc-p1-proj-tilde-functorial identifies the map
   with multiplication by F, using def-weil-pbc-proj-twisting-sheaf.
3. The final sheaf is i_*O_Z: on every positive homogeneous basic open its
   sections are the degree-zero part of the localized quotient S/(F).
   The quotient and restriction maps send each homogeneous fraction to its
   quotient fraction. They commute with localization and hence glue on the
   standard affine basis (def-pc-proj, def-weil-closed-immersion). On the three
   coordinate charts this is exactly the zero scheme used in `scheme`, whose
   ideal is (F/X_i^d). Thus 0 -> O(-d) -> O -> i_*O_Z -> 0 has its actual
   restriction map. No radical of (F) is taken.
4. Compare module and underlying abelian-sheaf cohomology on P and Z by
   lem-sc-zariski-module-abelian-cohomology-agree, then use
   lem-sc-cohomology-closed-immersion-pushforward. Forgetting modules commutes
   with direct image by the open-set formula. The comparisons are natural in
   scalar multiplication, so identify H^q(P,i_*O_Z) with H^q(Z,O_Z) k-linearly.
   In degree zero this is the equality of global sections, carrying the
   quotient sheaf map to restriction of functions.
5. Apply lem-pc-projective-space-twist-cohomology on P^2 with twists -d and 0.
   Since d is positive, H^0(O(-d))=H^1(O(-d))=0; also H^0(O)=k times 1
   and H^1(O)=H^2(O)=0. A basis of H^2(O(-d)) consists of Laurent monomials
   A^e0 B^e1 C^e2 with all exponents negative and their sum equal to -d.
6. Apply lem-weil-pbc-derived-functor-long-exact to global sections valued in
   k-vector spaces. Its source is abelian with enough injectives by
   lem-sc-ringed-space-module-sheaves-enough-injectives. Global sections is
   additive and left exact, and the same injective resolution computes
   def-sc-ringed-space-module-cohomology with scalars restricted to k.
   The exact segments are 0 -> 0 -> k -> H^0(Z,O_Z) -> 0 and
   0 -> H^1(Z,O_Z) -> H^2(O(-d)) -> 0. The first map is `scalarMap`,
   a unital ring map, so its bijection is a k-algebra isomorphism. The second
   is a k-linear isomorphism to the vector space with the basis in step 5.
7. Put a_i=-e_i-1. For d=1,2 there are no such nonnegative triples, and
   the displayed natural-number expression is zero. For d>=3 the sum of
   the a_i is m=d-3. For a_0=r there are m-r+1 choices for a_1, and a_2
   is determined. Summing over 0<=r<=m gives (m+1)(m+2)/2=(d-1)(d-2)/2.
   This is counting in the integers, not division by 2 in k. Neither the
   argument nor the construction requires reducedness, irreducibility,
   smoothness, or any characteristic restriction. -/
theorem cohomology_of_nonzero_homogeneous {k : Type u} [Field k]
    (d : ℕ) (hd : 1 ≤ d) (F : MvPolynomial (ULift.{u} (Fin 3)) k)
    (hF : F.IsHomogeneous d) (hF0 : F ≠ 0) :
    Function.Bijective (scalarMap d F hF) ∧
      Module.rank k (cohomology d F hF 1) = (((d - 1) * (d - 2) / 2 : ℕ) : Cardinal.{u}) := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry.PlaneHypersurface
